---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="About — RepMaths"
  description="Learn about RepMaths, our fitness calculators, and the established formulas and methods behind them."
>
  <article class="about-page">
    <h1>About RepMaths</h1>

    <p>
      RepMaths builds fast, free calculators for lifters and fitness
      enthusiasts — covering strength, training, equipment, nutrition,
      and body composition. No sign-up, no clutter, just useful estimates
      you can use right away.
    </p>

    <h2>Our Approach</h2>

    <p>
      RepMaths focuses on practical calculators rather than long-form
      articles. Each tool is designed to answer a specific fitness or
      strength-training question and provide a clear result with the
      method, formula, or assumptions used.
    </p>

    <p>
      Where established formulas or published methods are available, we
      use those rather than creating our own formulas. The exact method
      used is identified on the relevant calculator page so users can
      understand what the result represents.
    </p>

    <h2>Methods &amp; Formulas We Use</h2>

    <h3>Strength &amp; 1RM Estimation</h3>

    <ul>
      <li>Epley Formula</li>
      <li>Brzycki Formula</li>
      <li>Lander Formula</li>
      <li>Lombardi Formula</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Training &amp; Load Management</h3>

    <ul>
      <li>
        RPE (Rate of Perceived Exertion), using the RTS
        (Reactive Training Systems) RPE chart
      </li>
      <li>RIR (Reps in Reserve) method</li>
      <li>
        Training Max methodology used in 5/3/1-style programming
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Nutrition &amp; Energy Expenditure</h3>

    <ul>
      <li>Mifflin-St Jeor Equation for BMR and TDEE estimation</li>
      <li>Harris-Benedict Equation</li>
      <li>Standard TDEE activity multipliers</li>
      <li>
        Protein intake ranges informed by the ISSN
        (International Society of Sports Nutrition) position stand
        on protein and exercise
      </li>
      <li>
        Standard macronutrient calorie values:
        4 kcal/g for protein and carbohydrates, and 9 kcal/g for fat
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Body Composition</h3>

    <ul>
      <li>
        U.S. Navy Body Fat Method
        (Hodgdon &amp; Beckett, 1984)
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How to Interpret Our Results</h2>

    <p>
      Fitness calculations often depend on individual measurements,
      training status, body composition, and the assumptions built into
      each formula. For that reason, RepMaths results should generally
      be treated as estimates or practical starting points rather than
      exact measurements.
    </p>

    <p>
      Different formulas can produce different results from the same
      inputs. When a calculator supports multiple methods, the tool page
      explains the methods used so you can understand the differences.
    </p>

    <h2>Educational Use</h2>

    <p>
      RepMaths is designed for educational and informational purposes.
      Our calculators are not a substitute for professional medical,
      nutritional, or fitness advice.
    </p>

    <p>
      For important health, nutrition, or training decisions, consider
      consulting an appropriately qualified professional. See our
      <a href="/disclaimer">Disclaimer</a> for more information.
    </p>
  </article>

  <style>
    .about-page {
      margin-top: 32px;
    }

    .about-page h2 {
      font-size: 1.3rem;
      margin-top: 32px;
    }

    .about-page h3 {
      font-size: 1.05rem;
      margin-top: 22px;
      color: var(--text);
    }

    .about-page ul {
      color: #c7cbd2;
      font-size: 0.95rem;
      padding-left: 20px;
    }

    .about-page li {
      margin-bottom: 7px;
    }

    .about-page p {
      line-height: 1.7;
    }

    .about-page a {
      color: var(--accent);
    }
  </style>
</Base>
